  "Question": "Pat intended to multiply a number by \\(6\\) but instead divided by \\(6.\\) Pat then meant to add \\(14\\) but instead subtracted \\(14.\\) After these mistakes, the result was \\(16.\\) If the correct operations had been used, the value produced would have been",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "less than \\(400\\)",
    "B": "between \\(400\\) and \\(600\\)",
    "C": "between \\(600\\) and \\(800\\)",
    "D": "between \\(800\\) and \\(1000\\)",
    "E": "greater than \\(1000\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Undo the mistaken operations in reverse order to recover the starting number. Before Pat subtracted \\(14\\), the value was \\(16+14=30\\). Since this was the result of dividing the starting number by \\(6\\), the starting number was \\(30\\cdot6=180\\).\n\nUsing the intended operations gives\n\\[\n180\\cdot6+14=1080+14=1094.\n\\]\nThis is greater than \\(1000\\), so the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
